WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.464
A subagent is a separate worker, with its own context, tools and instructions.

00:00:05.814 --> 00:00:08.801
It does a side task and hands back only a summary.

00:00:10.201 --> 00:00:12.611
Say a question means reading forty files.

00:00:12.961 --> 00:00:16.247
In the main conversation, all forty would fill your context.

00:00:16.597 --> 00:00:20.189
Hand it to a subagent, and it reads them in its own window,

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:21.589
then returns ten lines.

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:26.696
It starts fresh, without your conversation's history, so your context barely moves.

00:00:27.046 --> 00:00:31.784
Claude Code ships with Explore, Plan, and a general-purpose agent.

00:00:31.784 --> 00:00:33.937
Your own go in .claude/agents.

00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:36.454
You can also ask for one directly:

00:00:36.454 --> 00:00:39.897
use a subagent to find every place the config is read.

00:00:41.297 --> 00:00:44.135
This portfolio has one called article writer.

00:00:44.485 --> 00:00:48.087
It's a Markdown file with a description, the tools it may use,

00:00:48.087 --> 00:00:52.677
and the house rules for every article: the structure, the tone, and the checks.

00:00:53.027 --> 00:00:57.357
Its description says when to hand off, so the main agent knows when to call it.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:02.448
Summaries lose detail, and you only see what it chose to report.

00:01:02.798 --> 00:01:07.897
It also spends more tokens overall, because each subagent builds its own context.

00:01:08.247 --> 00:01:12.065
It can't see what you've already read or decided, so give it a full brief.

00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:17.041
So use one when a side task would flood the conversation, not by default.

00:01:17.041 --> 00:01:20.970
The setup behind this series now defaults to the main session.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.627
Codex supports custom agents too, defined in TOML.
